WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.520
macOS unified logging is a major event source for modern Mac investigations.

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:10.820
Unified logs can contain system, application, security-relevant, and operational events across time.

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:20.640
They can help analysts understand application behavior, service activity, device state, authentication-related clues, installation events, and errors.

00:00:20.860 --> 00:00:31.020
The challenge is that unified logs can be high volume and require careful filtering by time range, process, subsystem, category, or keywords.

00:00:31.240 --> 00:00:35.000
Traditional logs and application-specific logs still matter.

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:38.580
Install logs can show software installation and update activity.

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:45.200
Application logs may record errors, user actions, synchronization behavior, or service activity.

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:55.020
Endpoint security products, management agents, VPN clients, browser tools, collaboration apps, and developer tools may all produce their own records.

00:00:55.240 --> 00:01:00.200
Investigators should know which logs are native, which are third-party, and which are centrally forwarded.

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:02.580
Retention and time ranges are important.

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:10.560
Older logs may be rotated, compressed, expired, overwritten, or unavailable depending on system settings and collection timing.

00:01:10.780 --> 00:01:18.140
Unified log availability can vary by macOS version, log level, privacy behavior, and whether the system is still live.

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:24.360
Missing logs are not automatic proof of tampering, but gaps should be documented and compared with other sources.

00:01:24.580 --> 00:01:26.980
Logs must be correlated with other evidence.

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:37.680
A log line can provide a useful lead, but it may be incomplete or ambiguous without file metadata, user artifacts, application data, endpoint telemetry, or cloud records.

00:01:37.900 --> 00:01:45.500
Good log analysis asks what the event shows, what it does not show, and what other evidence can confirm or challenge the interpretation.
